// THE INCREMENTAL TIER, AND THE THREE PLACES IT DISAGREES WITH THE OTHER TWO —
// PINNED AS CURRENT BEHAVIOUR, TWO OF THE FIVE ANSWERS WRONG ON PURPOSE.
//
// ⚠⚠ READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANY EXPECTATION IN THIS FILE. Two of the blocks
// below assert an answer that IS WRONG, and one asserts a REFUSAL that ought
// not to be a refusal. They are marked `⚠ KNOWN-WRONG` and each carries the
// answer it OUGHT to give. THE EXPECTATION IS THE DEFECT. When the arc that
// owns this — the query compiler's value domain becoming Logos's type system —
// closes any of them, THIS FILE MUST GO RED AND BE REWRITTEN. A red here from a
// fix is the file doing its job; do not "repair" it by re-reading the answer off
// a run. Same idiom, same reason, as
// `tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_avg_struct_arg_fail.logos`.
//
// ⚠ EVERY BLOCK CARRIES ITS OWN IN-RUN CONTROL, and that is not decoration. The
// three surveys behind this file each recorded the same lesson: a control that
// changes nothing is evidence about the STAND, not about the code. So each
// wrong answer sits beside the SAME question asked of a type that works, in the
// same program, over the same rows, through the same engine:
//   • `max` over u64 is wrong  — `max` over i64 in the same query is right;
//   • `sum` over f32 is wrong  — `sum` over f64 over the same magnitudes is right;
//   • an equi-join on an f64 key is REFUSED — an equi-join on an i64 key over
//     the same two sources is ACCEPTED, so the refusal is about the KEY TYPE and
//     not about the join shape, which is the only thing that makes it a finding.
// If a control ever goes red the file says nothing about the defect it was
// written for, and the exit code says which half broke.
//
// ── 1. `max` OVER `u64` ANSWERS THE SMALLER VALUE, IN BOTH RUNTIME TIERS ─────
// `wany_to_rt` (stdlib/mem/deem/deem.logos:779) has ONE integer arm,
// `RtVal::I(i64)`. Over the group {1u64, 18446744073709551615u64} the two values
// arrive as 1 and -1, so `max` answers 1 — the SMALLER of the two. Pinned here
// as 1. It OUGHT to answer 18446744073709551615, which `QRows::get_i64` cannot
// even spell (the five getters at stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos:68-78 are
// i64/f64/bool/str/node, none unsigned); the nearest honest statement of the
// right answer is "the bits -1". So when this is fixed BOTH the value and the
// read-back path have to move, and both lines here go red.
//
// ── 2. `sum` OVER `f32` ADDS TWO DENORMALS, IN BOTH RUNTIME TIERS ────────────
// `WAny::as_f64` (stdlib/lang/writ/anyval.logos:329) dereferences the boxed
// payload as `*const f64` without consulting the Ref's own type code, which
// distinguishes W_F32=30 from W_F64=31 and which `is_float()` at :303-307 DOES
// read. So 1.0f32 arrives as the f64 whose bits are 0x3F800000 = 1065353216 and
// 2.0f32 as bits 0x40000000 = 1073741824 — both far inside the subnormal range,
// where the significand IS the integer, so their sum is the integer sum:
//       1065353216 + 1073741824 = 2139095040 = 0x7F800000.
// That number is DERIVED from the mechanism, not read off a run; if the
// mechanism claim were wrong the arithmetic would not land on it. The answer
// OUGHT to be 3.0, whose f64 bits are 4613937818241073152 — which is exactly
// what the f64 control beside it produces over the same magnitudes.
//
// ── 3. AN EQUI-JOIN ON AN f64 KEY: DYNAMIC ACCEPTS, INCREMENTAL REFUSES ──────
// Same query text, same catalog, same QEnv, same two sources. The dynamic
// engine runs it and returns the matching row; `incremental()` returns Err. The
// static tier accepts an f64 join key too, so two of three engines allow it and
// the third does not — and it is NOT the join shape, because the i64-keyed join
// over the same two sources is accepted by the incremental engine in this same
// program. ⚠ WHERE the incremental engine refuses this was NOT established by
// the survey that measured it: `incremental()` returns Err with no message
// reaching the caller, and `agg_val_key`/`agg_ord_key` (stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos:572-585)
// are float-aware for min/max, so the join path has a separate rule nobody has
// located. This fixture pins THAT THE DISAGREEMENT EXISTS; it does not claim to
// know its cause, and a fix must rewrite block 3.
//
// ── 4. THE SHAPE FLOOR, WHICH IS NOT A TYPE FACT AND IS PINNED SO IT IS NOT ──
// ── MISTAKEN FOR ONE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `incremental()` refuses `select`, `where`, `order by` and `distinct` for EVERY
// type INCLUDING i64: only grouped aggregates and joins are incremental shapes.
// 238 of the survey's 1066 cells are that one fact wearing 238 different type
// labels. It is pinned here ONCE, on the i64 control, so that a reader of this
// arc does not spend a day attributing it to the value domain. ⚠ Whether this
// floor is a deliberate DBSP fragment boundary or an unwired shape is ALSO not
// established — ADR 0013 §3.1 and the `incr.logos` construction path would
// answer it. If the fragment grows, this block goes red and that is correct.
//
// ⚠ THE EXPECTATIONS ARE HAND-DERIVED CONSTANTS. Nothing here asks `el_ty`,
// `RtVal` or any survey artifact what the answer is, and nothing folds a sum
// with the arithmetic the engine folds it with — an oracle that shares the
// algorithm with the code under test agrees with itself by construction
// (`feedback_oracle_must_be_independent`).
//
// The correct-answer halves live in `wql_domain_static_extremes` (the static
// tier at the same extremes) and `wql_domain_runtime_extremes` (the dynamic
// tier, 18 types); the refusal-plus-LAYER halves live in `wql_domain_layer_*_fail`.
//
// exit 0 = the incremental tier is exactly as measured on 2026-08-03,
//          disagreements included.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.num.float;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema R : code(0x0E67_0000_0000_0001) {
    id: i64 = 0,
    g: i64 = 1,
    x_i64: i64 = 2,
    x_u64: u64 = 3,
    x_f32: f32 = 4,
    x_f64: f64 = 5
}
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ R };

fn mkr(d: &Writ, id: i64, g: i64, xi: i64, xu: u64, xf: f32, xd: f64) -> WAny {
    let r: R = d.make::<R>();
    r.id = id; r.g = g; r.x_i64 = xi; r.x_u64 = xu; r.x_f32 = xf; r.x_f64 = xd;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let d: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();

    // ONE group, TWO rows. The u64 pair straddles 2^63 — 1 and 2^64-1 — so a
    // signed read inverts their order; the f32/f64 pair is 1.0 and 2.0, exact in
    // both widths, so nothing here turns on rounding.
    let a: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    a.push(mkr(&d, 0i64, 0i64, 1i64, 1u64,                    1.0f32, 1.0f64));
    a.push(mkr(&d, 1i64, 0i64, 2i64, 18446744073709551615u64, 2.0f32, 2.0f64));
    // A SECOND, independently built source with one matching row per key type —
    // the join fragment wants two distinct base sources.
    let b: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(3i64);
    b.push(mkr(&d, 7i64, 0i64, 2i64, 1u64,                    1.0f32, 2.0f64));

    let mut e: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(a as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    e.bind_source("ts", WAny::ref_to(b as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));

    // ── 1. max(u64) — INCREMENTAL. ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: answers 1, the SMALLER of
    //      {1, 2^64-1}. OUGHT to answer 2^64-1, whose i64 bits are -1.
    //      CONTROL in the same query: max(i64) over {1, 2} must answer 2.
    let q1: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate m = max(s.x_u64), n = max(s.x_i64) select (key, m, n)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } };
    let g1: IncrJoin = match q1.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 11i32; } };
    if g1.had_error() { return 12i32; }
    let s1: QRows = g1.snapshot();
    if s1.row_count() != 1i64 { return 13i32; }
    if s1.get_i64(0i64, 2i64) != 2i64 { return 14i32; }   // CONTROL: max(i64) = 2
    // OUGHT: 18446744073709551615, whose bits read back through get_i64 are -1.
    // Reaching this arm means the defect CLOSED — rewrite the block, do not
    // re-read the number off a run.
    if s1.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) == -1i64 { return 15i32; }
    if s1.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 1i64 { return 16i32; }   // ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: the SMALLER value

    // ── 1b. the SAME question of the DYNAMIC engine — it agrees, wrongly.
    let q2: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate m = max(s.x_u64), n = max(s.x_i64) select (m, n)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 20i32; } };
    let r2: QRows = match q2.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 21i32; } };
    if r2.row_count() != 1i64 { return 22i32; }
    if r2.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 2i64 { return 23i32; }   // CONTROL: max(i64) = 2
    // OUGHT: 18446744073709551615 — bits -1 through get_i64. See block 1.
    if r2.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) == -1i64 { return 24i32; }
    if r2.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 25i32; }   // ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: the SMALLER value

    // ── 2. sum(f32) — INCREMENTAL. ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: adds two subnormals and
    //      answers bits 2139095040. OUGHT to answer 3.0, bits 4613937818241073152.
    //      CONTROL in the same query: sum(f64) over 1.0 + 2.0 must be exactly 3.0.
    let q3: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x_f32), b = sum(s.x_f64) select (key, a, b)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 30i32; } };
    let g3: IncrJoin = match q3.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 31i32; } };
    if g3.had_error() { return 32i32; }
    let s3: QRows = g3.snapshot();
    if s3.row_count() != 1i64 { return 33i32; }
    if s3.get_f64(0i64, 2i64) != 3.0f64 { return 34i32; } // CONTROL: sum(f64) = 3.0
    // OUGHT: 3.0, whose f64 bits are 4613937818241073152 — the same number the
    // f64 control on the line above produces. Reaching this arm means the defect
    // CLOSED; rewrite the block.
    if f64_to_bits(s3.get_f64(0i64, 1i64)) == 4613937818241073152u64 { return 35i32; }
    if f64_to_bits(s3.get_f64(0i64, 1i64)) != 2139095040u64 { return 36i32; }  // ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG

    // ── 2b. the SAME question of the DYNAMIC engine — it agrees, wrongly.
    let q4: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x_f32), b = sum(s.x_f64) select (a, b)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 40i32; } };
    let r4: QRows = match q4.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 41i32; } };
    if r4.row_count() != 1i64 { return 42i32; }
    if r4.get_f64(0i64, 1i64) != 3.0f64 { return 43i32; } // CONTROL: sum(f64) = 3.0
    // OUGHT: 3.0, bits 4613937818241073152. See block 2.
    if f64_to_bits(r4.get_f64(0i64, 0i64)) == 4613937818241073152u64 { return 44i32; }
    if f64_to_bits(r4.get_f64(0i64, 0i64)) != 2139095040u64 { return 45i32; }  // ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG

    // ── 3. AN EQUI-JOIN ON AN i64 KEY — the CONTROL. Both engines accept it, so
    //      the join shape itself is an incremental shape over these two sources.
    let q5: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss x join ts y on x.x_i64 == y.x_i64 select x.id", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 50i32; } };
    let r5: QRows = match q5.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 51i32; } };
    if r5.row_count() != 1i64 { return 52i32; }
    if r5.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 53i32; }
    match q5.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_g) => { }
        Result::Err(_e) => { return 54i32; }   // CONTROL: the incremental engine
                                               // DOES take an equi-join here.
    }

    // ── 3b. THE SAME JOIN ON AN f64 KEY. ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: the dynamic engine runs
    //      it and the incremental engine refuses it. Two of three engines accept
    //      an f64 join key (the static tier is the third); this one does not, and
    //      says nothing about why.
    let q6: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss x join ts y on x.x_f64 == y.x_f64 select x.id", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 60i32; } };
    let r6: QRows = match q6.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 61i32; } };  // DYNAMIC: accepts
    if r6.row_count() != 1i64 { return 62i32; }
    if r6.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 63i32; }
    match q6.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_g) => { return 64i32; }    // ⚠ KNOWN-WRONG: reaching here means
                                               // the disagreement CLOSED — rewrite
                                               // this block, do not delete it.
        Result::Err(_e) => { }
    }

    // ── 4. THE SHAPE FLOOR, on the i64 CONTROL — not a type fact. `select`,
    //      `where`, `order by` and `distinct` are refused by `incremental()` for
    //      EVERY type, i64 included; the dynamic engine takes all four.
    let q7: Query = match Query::compile("from ss s select s.x_i64", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 70i32; } };
    match q7.run(&e) { Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 71i32; } }
    match q7.incremental(&e) { Result::Ok(_g) => { return 72i32; } Result::Err(_e) => { } }

    let q8: Query = match Query::compile("from ss s select s.id order by s.x_i64", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 73i32; } };
    match q8.run(&e) { Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 74i32; } }
    match q8.incremental(&e) { Result::Ok(_g) => { return 75i32; } Result::Err(_e) => { } }

    let q9: Query = match Query::compile("from ss s where s.x_i64 < 2 select s.id", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 76i32; } };
    match q9.run(&e) { Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 77i32; } }
    match q9.incremental(&e) { Result::Ok(_g) => { return 78i32; } Result::Err(_e) => { } }

    return 0i32;
}
